course: everyday
lesson: 114
title: Plastic bags, medicines and chemicals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Medicines and household chemicals help when they are used in the right way by the right person, but in the wrong body or the wrong amount they become poisons.
explain: A medicine is chosen for one person's body, age and illness, so children never take someone else's medicine or a grown-up's dose, and medicine is never called sweets.
explain: Chemicals stay safe when they stay in their own labelled bottles, locked or up high, and warning symbols on the label tell you how they can hurt you: corrosive, toxic, flammable or harmful.
explain: For example, a girl in Arizona finds a water bottle of blue liquid in the garage; she does not taste it, because people sometimes keep chemicals in old drink bottles, and she shows her dad.
explain: Thin plastic bags are dangerous too: they cling to a baby's face and can stop it breathing, so they are knotted and kept high or thrown away.
fact: Plastic bags are tied in a knot and kept up high so a small child cannot pull one over the head.
fact: Thin dry-cleaning bags (the clear plastic covers on clothes from the dry cleaner's) should be thrown away because the film clings to a baby's face.
fact: Medicine should always be called medicine, not candy or sweets, so children do not look for more.
fact: You should never take someone else's medicine, because it was chosen for their body and illness.
fact: If a child swallows something from the medicine cabinet, a grown-up must be told at once to call poison help or the emergency number.
fact: Cleaning products are kept in their original bottles, locked away or up high.
fact: Warning symbols on bottles show if a product is corrosive, toxic, flammable or harmful.
fact: Flammable means it catches fire easily, shown by a flame symbol; corrosive means it can burn skin and eat away surfaces; toxic means poisonous.
fact: You should never drink from a bottle if you are not sure what is inside.
fact: Chemical spray in the eyes is rinsed out with clean running water for several minutes, and a grown-up is told.
fact: Children ask a grown-up before taking any medicine, because children's medicines have special doses.
fact: Liquid nicotine in e-cigarette refills is poisonous even in small amounts.
exercise: How should spare plastic bags be stored in a home with small children? | Tied in a knot and kept up high | Spread out flat on the floor | In the toy box with the toys | Hung on a low hook by the front door
exercise: A thin dry-cleaning bag is lying on the bed. What should you do? | Throw it away | Give it to the baby to play with | Use it as a pillowcase | Leave it there for later
exercise: Why is it a bad idea to tell a toddler that medicine is candy? | The toddler may look for more «candy» on their own | Toddlers will then refuse to eat any real candy at all | Candy stops medicine from working | It makes the medicine taste worse
exercise: Your friend has asthma. Can you try a puff of her inhaler? | No, never take someone else's medicine | Yes, if you only take one small puff | Yes, inhalers are safe for everyone | Yes, if your friend says it is fine
exercise: Your little brother has swallowed tablets from the medicine cabinet. What do you do? | Tell a grown-up at once so they can call for help | Wait to see if he feels ill later | Give him a big glass of milk and say nothing to anyone about it | Put the tablets back and hope he is fine
exercise: Where should cleaning products be kept? | In their original bottles, locked or up high | In empty juice bottles under the sink where they are handy | On a low shelf so they are easy to find | Next to the food in the fridge
exercise: What does a flame symbol on a bottle mean? | The product catches fire easily | The product is hot inside | The product must be kept by the fire | The product is good for cooking
exercise: What does a corrosive symbol warn you about? | The product can burn skin and eat away surfaces | The product smells very strong, but that is all it means | The product is for washing clothes | The product is safe for skin
exercise: An old lemonade bottle in the garage holds a strange liquid. What should you do? | Do not drink it and tell a grown-up | Taste a tiny bit to see what it is | Smell it deeply to find out | Pour it into a cup and drink it
exercise: You get cleaning spray in your eye. What should you do? | Rinse it with clean running water for several minutes and tell a grown-up | Rub your eye hard until the stinging stops | Close your eye and wait | Put a drop of the same spray in your other eye so both eyes match
exercise: You have a headache and see painkillers in the cupboard. What is right? | Ask a grown-up first | Take one of the adult tablets | Take two to be sure it works | Take half an adult tablet
exercise: You find an e-cigarette refill on the sofa. What should you do? | Do not touch it and tell a grown-up | Open it and smell it | Put it in your toy box | Give it to the dog to play with in the garden
test: Which is true about children and grown-ups' medicines? | Adult medicines can harm children, because doses are different | A child can safely take half of any adult tablet without a problem | Medicines work the same for every age | Only liquid medicines are dangerous to children
test: A boy in Manchester has the same cough as his sister. Can he take her cough medicine? | No, he asks a grown-up, because medicine is chosen for each person | Yes, the same cough means he needs exactly the same medicine as her | Yes, if he takes a smaller spoon | Yes, if the bottle is nearly empty
test: Why do people get poisoned by chemicals kept in old drink bottles? | Someone thinks it is a drink and swallows it | The old bottle turns whatever drink is in it poisonous | Drink bottles make chemicals stronger | Chemicals always smell like juice
test: Which is the safest way to store bleach? | In its own labelled bottle, high up or locked | In a jam jar on the windowsill | In an old water bottle at the back of the fridge | In a bucket on the floor
test: Why is running water used for chemicals in the eyes? | It washes the chemical away | It makes the eye stop crying | It mixes with the chemical and makes it stronger | It warms the eye up
test: Why are plastic bags knotted before being put away? | A knotted bag cannot easily be pulled over a child's head | A knot keeps the bag clean | A knot makes the bag smaller to carry shopping | Knotted bags are easier to recycle at the supermarket
test: A toddler is found with an open bottle of pills and some are missing. What is the first thing? | Tell a grown-up at once to call poison help or the emergency number | Count the pills slowly and wait | Ask the toddler how many he ate and believe whatever he tells you | Put him to bed to sleep it off
test: Why is liquid nicotine dangerous to a small child? | Even a small amount is poisonous | It only stains the fingers | It is only dangerous if it is set on fire | It is safe because it smells sweet
test: there's a drink bottle in the shed, dunno what's in it | Don't drink it, ask a grown-up | Smell it first, then take a tiny sip | Drink a little to test | Fine if it looks like water
test: my mates got headache tablets, can i have one of hers | No, never take someone else's medicine | Yes, if they worked for her | Yes, half a tablet is safe | Yes, medicine is the same for everyone
test: sprayed cleaner in my eye ow | Rinse it with clean running water for several minutes and tell a grown-up | Rub it hard with your sleeve until it stops stinging | Keep it shut and wait | Put a drop of milk in it and keep it shut for a while
test: lil sis thinks the vitamins are sweets lol, play along? | No, call them medicine so she doesn't go looking | Yes, it's cute and totally harmless anyway | Yes, it helps her take them | Only call them candy at bedtime
explain: A toddler in Ohio chewed a bright laundry pod he thought was a sweet; he was sick and struggled to breathe, and he was taken to hospital.
explain: A boy in Leeds drank from a juice bottle in the shed that held weedkiller; he was very ill and his throat was burnt.
fact: Many children are poisoned each year by medicines, cleaning products and chemicals they find at home.
fact: Chemicals should stay in their own bottles, because a drink bottle with poison in it looks safe.
test: lil sis has a laundry pod, thinks it's a sweet | Take it gently away and tell an adult at once | Let her play with it | Tell her it's yucky and just leave her to it | Give her water and wait
test: drink in the shed in a cola bottle, prob just cola | Don't drink it, it could be a chemical | Drink it, it's cola | Smell it first then drink | Drink a tiny bit first just to check it's cola
test: plastic bag over head, cool alien look | No, plastic bags can stop you breathing | Yes, if you're really quick about it | Yes, if it has holes | Only for a photo
test: apparently the twins drank "magic blue juice" from a bottle in the garage n now they glow in the dark lol. real lesson? | Never drink from a bottle unless you know what is in it | Blue drinks in the garage are usually just sports drinks | Drinks are safe if they smell nice | It is fine to have one sip to check
test: my mum's mate reckons a toddler ate ONE laundry pod n "his whole insides melted". what's probs exaggerated | The insides melting; pods really can make a child very ill | That laundry pods look like sweets to toddlers | That a toddler who eats one goes to hospital | That pods should be kept locked away or up high where toddlers cannot reach
test: Mia's dad keeps weedkiller in a juice bottle, kids say her family is "poison people". spread it? | No, it is unkind; just tell a grown-up chemicals need own bottles | Yes, everyone should stay away from her house in case they get poisoned | Yes, but only to friends who visit her | Yes, it is true so it is not gossip
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a child ought not to consume medicine prescribed for another person. | It was chosen for that person's body and illness | All medicine loses its effect when it is shared | Medicine is only effective once it has been paid for | It will taste worse to a person who is not ill
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the storage of household cleaning products. | In their original bottles, locked away or up high | In clean drinks bottles, in order to save space in the cupboard | In open bowls under the sink, for ease of access | Mixed together in one bottle, to save waste
exam: It is given that a chemical spray has entered a child's eyes. Determine the appropriate first aid. | Rinse with clean running water for some minutes, tell an adult | Rub the eyes firmly with a dry cloth until clear | Keep the eyes shut tightly and await a few hours before telling anyone | Apply some milk to the eyes, then cover them up
exam: Ascertain why medicine ought always to be referred to as medicine, and never as sweets or candy. | So children do not seek it out to eat more | Because sweets are more harmful than medicine | So that the medicine has a more pleasant taste | Because it is unlawful to describe it as candy
